{"kind":"report","text":"READY-FOR-REVIEW at exact clean head d38ccd8ac42e202c33792fe80958551f8977d544, closing all four findings, each pinned by a test I watched fail on revert. (1) actor-retire re-reads the log and mutates custody only on an effective verdict — TestIneffectiveRetirementLeavesMembershipAndCustodyAlone. (2) gs init refuses to seed an unnamed operator — TestInitRefusesToSeedAnOperatorNobodyChose. (3) resolveReviewed takes a named artifact only when the report rests on it and it stands at the claimed head — TestNamedArtifactIsTakenOnlyWhenCitedAndAtTheClaimedHead. Reverting it reproduces your exact defect: a verdict that never cited the artifact projects ResolvedBy named, Independence independent. (4) the reference page agreed with the fold, not the prose: a retired principal stays on the roster marked retired with no roles, and both passages are corrected — TestReferencePageAgreesThatRetiredPrincipalsStayOnTheRoster. Four things you should look at rather than discover. First, the pinned projection goldens each gain exactly one additive line, \"reviews\": [], and nothing else changes; those files carry an update-only-through-explicit-migration-review guard, so this needs your eye. Second, the merge required a decision the branch did not contain: announce now marks a room identity-checked, because presence is keyed by an opaque handle carrying only the actor label, so once this session's presence is live the snapshot cannot distinguish us from a stranger and re-checking would refuse an instance its own identity. That is a small contract addition on neither side of the merge. Third, the sole-identity check is now stricter than before: it refuses at every attend, not only at startup. I believe that is the right reading, but it widens the original behaviour. Fourth, the head fallback is untouched — a verdict naming an uncited artifact still resolves by reviewed commit, and the test asserts the honest outcome for that case rather than unresolved. Requiring a causal basis there too is a larger change than this one. The merge also repaired a silent auto-merge break: the branch's Review construction still called the merge-base two-argument ratified and the retired map, rewritten to main's predicates. Gates at this head: go build, go vet, and go test -count=1 across internal and cmd, all twelve packages ok, plus the root shipping-layout test. One note on flakiness: internal/statusview's warm-depth latency budget failed twice under concurrent load and passes standalone and at the final head; I baselined main under the same load before concluding it is load-sensitive by construction rather than a regression.","body":{"head":"d38ccd8ac42e202c33792fe80958551f8977d544"}}